# forge:hash=7c0c5c0bb2a4c734f08eb6ba5e567f4e7f9fc7f15460790359a830ae710a1f81
# ── Go build outputs ──
bin/
dist/
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
*.test
*.out
coverage.html
coverage/
vendor/

# Go profiling
*.prof
*.pprof

# ── Go build cache — a project-local GOCACHE ──
# Agents working in parallel worktrees point GOCACHE at $WORKTREE/.gocache so
# concurrent builds don't contend on one shared cache. It is machine-local
# build output (tens of thousands of files), and `git add -A` would otherwise
# sweep it into a commit.
#
# `forge lint` does not depend on this rule — its generated-file scan skips
# any directory carrying Go's GOCACHE sentinel README, whatever its name
# (internal/commitpolicy.IsGoBuildCacheDir) — but the rule still belongs here
# so the cache never reaches a commit in forge's own repo.
.gocache/

# Go workspace — local, per-developer dev-loop wiring; NEVER committed.
# The root go.mod already carries `replace <module>/gen => ./gen`, so a
# plain `go build ./...` resolves root↔gen with no go.work at all. A
# committed go.work is therefore both redundant AND a pollution hazard:
# the moment someone adds a sibling `use ../forge` (to build against a
# local, unpublished forge), that absolute/relative path breaks every
# other clone and CI. Keep it local. `forge new` scaffolds a starter
# go.work (`use ( . gen )`) for workspace mode on this machine — and
# `forge project new --link-forge` also adds a `use <local-forge>` so the
# project builds against your in-development forge checkout. Either way it
# stays ignored. go.work.sum is a generated lock file.
go.work
go.work.sum

# ── Node.js / Next.js ──
node_modules/
.next/
.turbo/
.vercel/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Handler-level generated code — CRUD handlers (Tier-1; forge
# regenerates these on every run).
handlers/**/*_gen.go
# NOTE: `handlers/**/*_gen_test.go` is intentionally NOT ignored. The
# current CRUD test scaffold (`handlers_crud_test.go`) is yours from line
# one — scaffolded once, never touched again — and MUST be committed.
# Legacy projects may still carry a retired `handlers_crud_gen_test.go`
# with `FORGE_SCAFFOLD:` markers; forge refreshes that file only while
# markers remain, and it becomes yours the moment the markers are cleared.
# Either way, committing it keeps `go test ./...` green on a fresh clone.

# Middleware generated code — auth
pkg/middleware/*_gen.go

# Service mocks (regenerated by forge generate).
#
# Anchor the patterns to the project root so they only match the Go
# handler-mock outputs at `/mocks/` and `/handlers/mocks/`. An
# unanchored `mocks/` rule also matches frontend mock directories
# (e.g. `frontends/<name>/src/mocks/scenarios/`), silently hiding new
# scenario files from git — see ADR 0002 on frontend scenarios.
/mocks/
/handlers/mocks/

# Seed data materializes at RUNTIME (`forge db seed` / `forge env up`
# auto-seed) — forge writes no seed files into the project. db/seeds/custom/
# is your hand-authored demo-data overlay and IS committed. Legacy
# auto-generated db/seeds/*.sql / db/fixtures/ from older forge versions can
# be deleted.

# ── IDE ──
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# ── Secrets ──
# secrets/<env>/ is the DirSecrets store deploy/kcl/<env>/main.k declares:
# one file per secret, filename = env-var name. forge resolves `sensitive`
# config fields (DATABASE_URL, …) from it for host runs and for the
# local-cluster Secret it renders. It must never reach git.
#
# Populate it with `forge secret set <env> <KEY>` — never by hand. A value
# only reaches a service that DECLARES it via EnvVar.secret_ref, so a file
# nothing references is inert; non-secret config belongs in
# deploy/kcl/<env>/config.k where it stays version-controlled.
#
# Exclude the CHILDREN (`secrets/*`), not the directory: git never
# descends into an excluded directory, so `secrets/` would make the
# .gitkeep negation below unreachable and the layout invisible in a
# fresh clone.
secrets/*
!secrets/*/
secrets/*/*
!secrets/*/.gitkeep

# ── Environment ──
# forge projects do NOT use .env files: their contents bypassed KCL (the
# whole file was injected into every host service), which is what pulled
# config out of version control. Ignored so a stray one can never be
# committed; `forge lint` fails on any that appear.
.env
.env.*

# ── OS ──
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ── Logs ──
*.log

# ── Local data / state ──
# Never commit on-disk databases, Docker volumes, or temp dirs.
data/
tmp/
*.air.toml.bak

# ── Forge internal state ──
# .forge/ contains debug-session.json, caches, .forge/state/build-<env>.json
# (handoff from `forge env build --push` to `forge env deploy`), transient side
# renders (.forge/render/ holds `--explain-drift` diff inputs), and
# other runtime state. Generated files certify THEMSELVES (each carries
# an embedded `forge:hash` marker), so there is no generated-state
# manifest to commit. Three exceptions are negated back in and MUST be
# committed when they exist:
#   disowned.json   — one-way ownership transfers (`forge disown`):
#                     path + reason + timestamp. Travels with the repo
#                     so forge never regenerates a disowned file in any
#                     clone or worktree.
#   hashes.json     — scoped render hashes for the few generated files
#                     whose format cannot carry an embedded marker
#                     (JSON outputs only).
#   scaffolded.json — the scaffold-once BIRTH LEDGER: every "yours" file
#                     forge has ever scaffolded here. It is what makes
#                     DELETING a scaffold stick — without it, an absent
#                     file is indistinguishable from one never written
#                     and the next `forge generate` hands it back. Must
#                     travel with the repo: a teammate's fresh clone has
#                     to inherit "we removed that file", not re-acquire
#                     it on their first generate.
# Everything else under .forge/ is per-developer state and intentionally
# excluded by the broad children rule below.
#
# NOTE: this must be `.forge/*` (children), not `.forge/` (the dir):
# git cannot re-include a file whose parent DIRECTORY is excluded, so a
# `.forge/` + `!.forge/<file>` pair would silently ignore the file and
# it would never get committed. (Found the hard way in cp-forge — see
# its .gitignore fork note.)
.forge/*
!.forge/disowned.json
!.forge/hashes.json
!.forge/scaffolded.json

# ── What NOT to ignore ──
#
# The following are scaffolded once by `forge new` and then owned by you.
# `forge upgrade` uses checksum protection (not .gitignore) to detect and
# preserve your edits. Commit them.
#
#   cmd/*.go            — entrypoints (package main)
#   pkg/middleware/*.go  — non-generated middleware (scaffolded, user-owned)
#   CHANGELOG.md         — project changelog
#   docs/                — ADRs, runbook, SLO definitions
#
# The following encode shared project conventions for LLM-driven
# development and are INTENTIONALLY committed:
#
#   .reliant/                           — forge-managed conventions, project metadata, skills
#   reliant.md / CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md  — user-owned project memory file (depends on --memory flag)
#   .cursorrules                        — project memory file (cursor format)
#   .github/copilot-instructions.md     — project memory file (copilot format)
#   .mcp.json                           — project-level MCP server config
#   .mcp.json.example                   — documentation of opt-in MCP servers
#
# Do not add these to .gitignore. If you need to keep local-only overrides,
# create a `.mcp.local.json` or similar and ignore that instead.

# ── This repository only: forge generates forge ──
#
# Everything below is specific to the forge repo itself and has no
# analogue in a scaffolded project, which is why it is not in the
# template. Keep it when adopting a newer template render.

# KCL resolves module deps into a lock file next to each kcl.mod. The
# forge repo carries several KCL modules (kcl/, kcl/example/,
# kcl/tests/) whose locks are machine-resolved and untracked.
**/kcl.mod.lock

# The template's `/mocks/` rule is root-anchored, so it no longer
# catches these — but forge SHIPS mock templates as source under
# internal/templates/frontend/mocks/. Negated explicitly so no future
# broadening of a `mocks/` rule can silently untrack forge's own
# template sources (all 7 are committed).
!internal/templates/frontend/mocks/
!internal/templates/frontend/mocks/**

# ── Claude Code runtime artifacts ──
# .claude/ is generated output, not source: `forge generate` renders the whole
# tree (83 skill files) from internal/templates, deterministically, on every
# run. Committing it means every generate produces a diff, which is what left
# `Verify Generated Code` failing on main with an untracked `?? .claude/`.
# Regenerate it instead of tracking it.
.claude/
.scratch/

# Local-only patterns for the forbidden-strings pre-commit guard (never committed)
.forbidden-strings

# The `forge` binary built into the repo root by a bare `go build`.
# Root-anchored so it cannot match the many `forge` DIRECTORIES in the
# tree (e.g. internal/templates/project/skills/forge/).
/forge
.grepai/
